[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     18[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     19[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     20[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Input classes that follow from this: no input, one byte, matches vs near
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     21[0m:[38;5;10m[49m misses ('a', 'X'), newlines (irrelevant: `fread`, not `fgets`), an embedded NUL
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (the buffer is zero-initialised, so the first NUL ends the string `foo` walks),
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m bytes above 0x7F, exactly 999 bytes, exactly 1000 bytes, more than 1000 bytes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (the excess is never read), stdin arriving in several short reads, stdin closed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m or unreadable, and stdout that cannot be written.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Mismatches found and fixed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 1. SIGPIPE: Rust exited 0 where the C was killed by signal 13
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Observed with stdout connected to a pipe whose reader had already exited:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | | exit status |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |---|---|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | C | killed by SIGPIPE (signal 13) |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Rust (before) | exited 0, no stderr |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Rust (after) | killed by SIGPIPE (signal 13) |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Cause: the Rust standard library sets `SIGPIPE` to `SIG_IGN` before `main`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m runs, so the failing `write` returned `EPIPE` (which this program ignores, like
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the C ignores `printf`'s return value) instead of killing the process. A C
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m program starts with the default disposition, so `printf`/`fflush` to a dead
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m pipe terminates it.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Fix: `restore_default_sigpipe()` in `src/main.rs` calls `signal(SIGPIPE,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m SIG_DFL)` as the first statement of `main`. stdout and stderr bytes were
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m already identical; only the exit status differed, which is exactly the failure
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m mode that a stdout-only comparison would have missed.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Covered by `stdout_is_a_pipe_with_no_reader`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 2. Input that exactly fills the buffer reads out of bounds in the C
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Observed once, with a 1200-byte file on stdin (first 1000 bytes = 700 'A' then
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 300 'x'):
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | | stdout |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |---|---|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | C | `A: 700\nx: 301\n` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Rust | `A: 700\nx: 300\n` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Cause: this is undefined behaviour in the C, not a translation defect.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `fread(in, 1, sizeof(in), stdin)` may fill all 1000 bytes of `in`, consuming
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the slot that held the zero initialiser, so the buffer is no longer
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m NUL-terminated and `strchr` walks past its end into whatever the stack holds
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m after it. In that one run the byte just past the buffer happened to be `0x78`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ('x'), producing the extra count.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Notes on reproducibility: the deviation was seen with a binary built as
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release` (-O3). With the build the instructions specify
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (plain `cmake ..`), the byte after the buffer has been 0 in every run measured
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - ~6000 runs across file-backed and pipe-backed stdin, at both -O0 and -O3,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   always `x: 300`. glibc zeroes the low byte of the stack canary, which is the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   most likely reason the read stops immediately in practice.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Resolution: the Rust program implements the well-defined interpretation, which
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m is also the behaviour the C exhibits whenever that out-of-bounds byte is 0: the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m string ends at the first NUL byte **or** at the end of the 1000-byte buffer,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m whichever comes first. No Rust change could reproduce arbitrary stack residue
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m from the C process, so this input class remains inherently unstable on the C
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m side and is recorded here rather than papered over. Tests covering it
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (`length_1000_fills_the_buffer`, `length_1000_mixed`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `length_1001_drops_the_last_byte`, `longer_than_buffer_is_truncated`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `much_longer_than_buffer`, `matches_only_beyond_the_buffer_are_invisible`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `stdin_delivered_in_small_slow_chunks`, `stdin_is_a_regular_file`, and the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `n >= 1000` cases of `length_sweep_around_the_boundary`) compare strictly and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m pass.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Behaviours checked and already matching
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Empty stdin, `/dev/null`, an empty regular file, and stdin closed outright
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   (`fread` fails, the zeroed buffer is used, output `A: 0\nx: 0\n`, exit 0).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Newlines, CRLF and whitespace are ordinary bytes: `fread` does not stop at
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   them.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Case sensitivity: 'a' and 'X' are not counted.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Adjacent matches: the `s++` after a hit still counts runs like `AAAA`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - A NUL anywhere in the first 1000 bytes hides everything after it.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Bytes 0x80..0xFF and invalid UTF-8 pass through without affecting the counts
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   (the Rust works on `[u8]`, never `str`).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Input longer than 1000 bytes: the remainder is never read, and unread
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   matches are not counted.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - stdin delivered in 25-byte chunks with pauses: both keep reading until the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   buffer is full or the stream ends (a single `read` per program would have
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   under-counted).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - stdout that always fails (`/dev/full`): both ignore the write error, print
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   nothing to stderr and exit 0.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Command-line arguments are ignored by both.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Output format is exactly `A: %d\nx: %d\n`, both lines newline-terminated,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   stderr always empty, exit code always 0 (absent a signal).
